<p>
  A <strong>process calibrator</strong> is an instrument that generates and/or measures precise electrical signals — mA, mV, V, and simulated temperature sensor signals — to verify that field instrumentation (transmitters, PLCs, chart recorders, indicators) is reading and controlling correctly. Where a multimeter tells you what a circuit is doing, a calibrator lets you inject a known, traceable reference signal and check the instrument's response against it. This guide covers what process calibrators do, the signal types involved, and how to choose one.
</p>

<h2 id="why-calibrate">Why Process Instrumentation Needs Calibration</h2>

<p>
  Field transmitters, indicators, and control loops drift over time due to component ageing, temperature effects, and mechanical wear. A pressure transmitter reading 2% high doesn't announce itself — the process still runs, just against a wrong number. Scheduled calibration against a traceable reference is how that drift gets caught before it causes a quality problem, a safety incident, or an unplanned shutdown. In regulated industries (pharma, food, oil & gas), calibration records are also a compliance requirement, not just good practice.
</p>

<h2 id="source-vs-measure">Source, Measure, and Simulate</h2>

<p>Process calibrators operate in three modes, and most instruments combine two or more:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Source (generate)</strong>: Outputs a precise signal — say, a fixed 12.000 mA current or a 4.00 mV signal — that feeds into the device under test so you can check its display or downstream response.</li>
  <li><strong>Measure</strong>: Reads an incoming signal from a live transmitter or loop with reference-grade accuracy, used to verify what the field device is actually outputting.</li>
  <li><strong>Simulate</strong>: Mimics the output of a sensor — most commonly a thermocouple or RTD — so a PLC, indicator, or controller downstream can be checked without needing the physical sensor connected.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="4-20ma-loop">The 4–20 mA Loop — The Industry Standard</h2>

<p>
  The 4–20 mA current loop is the dominant signal standard in process instrumentation, and it's why "mA calibrator" and "process calibrator" are often used interchangeably. Using current rather than voltage makes the signal immune to voltage drop over long cable runs — the same current flows through every point in a series loop regardless of wire resistance, so the reading at the receiving end is accurate even over hundreds of metres of cabling.
</p>

