<Callout type="info" title="Why 4 mA and not 0 mA for zero">
  If the signal range started at 0 mA, there would be no way to distinguish "the process is genuinely at zero" from "the wire is cut" or "the transmitter has lost power" — both would read 0 mA. Starting the live range at 4 mA means anything below that is unambiguously a fault, which is why it's called a live-zero signal.
</Callout>

<h2 id="temperature-simulation">Thermocouple and RTD Simulation</h2>

<p>
  Temperature calibrators go a step further than mA/mV sourcing — they simulate the exact millivolt or resistance output a real thermocouple or RTD would produce at a chosen temperature, referencing the correct standard curve for that sensor type (J, K, T, R, S thermocouples; Pt100, Pt1000 RTDs). This lets a technician check a temperature transmitter's calibration at, say, 500°C without needing a furnace or ice bath — the calibrator's output looks electrically identical to what the real sensor would produce.
</p>

<h2 id="accuracy-traceability">Accuracy and Traceability</h2>

<p>
  A calibrator's own accuracy must be meaningfully better than the accuracy of the device it's calibrating — as a rule of thumb, at least 4 times better (a 4:1 test uncertainty ratio) so the calibrator's own error doesn't swallow the pass/fail margin. The calibrator itself should carry a valid calibration certificate traceable to a national standards body; an out-of-calibration calibrator invalidates every calibration performed with it.
</p>

<h2 id="choosing">Choosing a Process Calibrator</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Loop-powered vs. self-powered</strong>: Confirm whether the calibrator can power a 2-wire loop itself, or whether it needs an external loop supply — this determines what you can test without extra equipment.</li>
  <li><strong>Signal types supported</strong>: mA, mV, V, and thermocouple/RTD simulation cover most plant instrumentation; some applications also need frequency, resistance, or pressure modules.</li>
  <li><strong>Documenting vs. non-documenting</strong>: Documenting calibrators log pass/fail results and generate calibration certificates automatically — valuable in regulated environments, unnecessary overhead for a general maintenance bench.</li>
  <li><strong>Portability</strong>: Field calibration work (checking a transmitter in situ on a process line) favours a rugged, battery-powered handheld unit over a bench instrument.</li>
</ul>

